q('After repeated ferric carboxymaltose infusions, a patient develops fatigue, proximal weakness and bone pain despite improved Hb. Which adverse effect is particularly important to assess?', 'Hypophosphatemia with possible hypophosphatemic bone disease',[
 ('Progressive iron deficiency as the only possible explanation','The post-infusion symptoms can reflect a separate treatment complication.'),('Hemolysis established by bone pain alone','Additional hemolysis evidence is required.'),('Hypercalcemia as the characteristic boxed-warning toxicity','The key current warning concerns low phosphate.')],
 'A corrected hemoglobin can coexist with a clinically important treatment harm', '**Ferric carboxymaltose can cause symptomatic hypophosphatemia.**',['Repeated exposure and predisposing nutritional or absorption problems can increase concern.','Prolonged phosphate depletion can cause osteomalacia and fractures.'], '**Apply the updated 2026 boxed warning and monitoring guidance.**',['Assess phosphate and relevant risk factors, especially around repeat courses.','Do not dismiss persistent fatigue as a need for more iron without reassessment.'],[['Symptoms','Weakness, bone pain and persistent fatigue'],['Test','Serum phosphate and relevant metabolic assessment'],['Warning','Symptomatic hypophosphatemia']],[FC,FL],style='clinical_scenario')
q('During an IV iron infusion, a patient develops wheeze, hypotension and generalized urticaria. What is the priority?', 'Stop the infusion and treat anaphylaxis promptly, including intramuscular epinephrine and supportive care',[
 ('Continue the infusion more rapidly to complete the dose','This risks worsening a life-threatening reaction.'),('Give oral iron and discharge immediately','The acute circulatory and airway features need emergency care.'),('Treat with an antihistamine alone despite hypotension and wheeze','This does not adequately treat anaphylaxis.')],
 'Recognize a systemic hypersensitivity emergency', '**Airway or circulatory compromise requires an anaphylaxis response.**',['Stop the suspected trigger.','Call for help and support airway, breathing and circulation.'], '**Differentiate later, after immediate treatment.**',['Some infusion reactions are not true anaphylaxis, but this presentation has high-risk features.','Document the event and review future replacement options with appropriate expertise.'],[['Trigger','IV infusion'],['Danger features','Wheeze and hypotension'],['Immediate treatment','Anaphylaxis protocol']],[FL],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An adult with marked iron deficiency has platelets 650 × 10^9/L without other myeloproliferative features. Which interpretation is appropriate initially?', 'Reactive thrombocytosis is possible, with reassessment after treating deficiency and evaluating the cause',[
 ('The platelet count alone proves essential thrombocythemia','Reactive causes must be assessed.'),('Iron deficiency necessarily causes thrombocytopenia only','Thrombocytosis is a recognized association.'),('The platelet count eliminates the need to investigate blood loss','It does not explain the iron deficit.')],
 'A high platelet count can accompany a nutritional or inflammatory process', '**Iron deficiency is an important reactive cause.**',['Review inflammation, infection and other secondary drivers.','Persistent thrombocytosis despite correction may require a clonal work-up.'], '**Avoid assigning a myeloproliferative diagnosis from one count.**',['Treat the deficiency and establish its cause.','Repeat the blood count and interpret the trajectory.'],[['Initial possibility','Reactive thrombocytosis'],['Intervention','Correct deficiency and investigate loss'],['Persistent abnormality','Further hematological assessment']],[BS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has combined iron deficiency and vitamin B12 deficiency but an MCV of 89 fL. Which interpretation is correct?', 'Opposing cell-size effects can produce a normal average MCV despite significant mixed deficiency',[
 ('A normal MCV excludes both deficiencies','The average can conceal a mixed population.'),('Only acute hemolysis can produce this MCV','Many disorders are normocytic or mixed.'),('A normal MCV proves the smear must be normal','Anisocytosis and dimorphic cells can still be present.')],
 'An average can conceal two abnormal populations', '**Microcytic and macrocytic processes can offset each other numerically.**',['RDW and the smear may reveal heterogeneity.','A reticulocyte response and nutrient studies add information.'], '**Treat confirmed deficiencies together with attention to B12 safety.**',['Neurological B12 disease may occur without macrocytosis.','Do not use folate alone to mask hematological evidence of untreated B12 deficiency.'],[['Iron deficiency','Smaller cells'],['B12 deficiency','Larger megaloblastic cells'],['Combined','Average MCV can look normal']],[BS,RI],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Iron deficiency recurs after appropriate iron replacement and adequate negative upper and lower GI endoscopy. What is an important next principle?', 'Assess the small bowel and renal tract when clinically indicated, with capsule endoscopy a key small-bowel option',[
 ('Assume no pathological source can remain','Small-bowel and nongastrointestinal causes may be missed by standard endoscopy.'),('Repeat iron forever without checking recurrence mechanisms','Recurrent or refractory disease needs renewed evaluation.'),('Use a negative IGRA as exclusion of GI bleeding','This test does not address that question.')],
 'A negative first-line investigation does not explain recurrent deficiency', '**Confirm the quality and timing of prior studies.**',['Review ongoing menstrual, urinary or other loss.','Small-bowel lesions or malabsorption may require targeted evaluation.'], '**Match further investigation to recurrence and response.**',['Uncomplicated first presentations can have an iron trial before routine capsule evaluation.','Recurrent or refractory cases differ from that initial low-risk pathway.'],[['Initial uncomplicated negative work-up','Iron trial may be appropriate'],['Recurrent / refractory','Further evaluation'],['Small bowel','Capsule is an important option']],[BS,GI],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A battery-recycling worker has abdominal colic, neuropathic symptoms and anemia with basophilic stippling. Which test is most directly relevant?', 'Venous blood lead concentration',[
 ('Serum B12 alone','It does not address the occupational toxic exposure pattern.'),('A single normal ferritin as exclusion of toxicity','Iron stores do not exclude lead exposure.'),('Direct antiglobulin test as the definitive lead assay','DAT tests an immune red-cell mechanism, not blood lead.')],
 'Exposure history can connect hematology with neurological and abdominal findings', '**Lead interferes with heme synthesis and can cause basophilic stippling.**',['Stippling is neither universally present nor specific.','Anemia may be microcytic or normocytic.'], '**Confirm exposure and remove its source.**',['Use current toxicology guidance for treatment decisions.','Evaluate renal, neurological and occupational implications.'],[['Exposure','Battery recycling'],['Mechanism','Heme synthesis interference'],['Confirmation','Blood lead measurement']],[LD],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What defines a ring sideroblast?', 'An erythroid precursor with iron-laden mitochondria arranged around the nucleus on an appropriate iron stain',[
 ('A mature red cell with a Heinz body','That is a different inclusion and cell stage.'),('A neutrophil with toxic granulation','This is not an erythroid mitochondrial iron pattern.'),('A circulating reticulocyte with no marrow iron','The defining finding is iron accumulation in a marrow erythroblast.')],
 'Iron may be present but not effectively incorporated into heme', '**Perinuclear mitochondrial iron produces the ring pattern.**',['Sideroblastic anemia can be inherited, toxic, nutritional or clonal.','The MCV is not uniformly low across all causes.'], '**Find the cause rather than treating every case as iron deficiency.**',['Review alcohol, medications, copper status and marrow features.','Clonal SF3B1-associated disease follows a separate diagnostic framework.'],[['Cell','Erythroid precursor'],['Compartment','Mitochondrial iron'],['Assessment','Inherited, acquired reversible or clonal cause']],[SI])
q('A patient develops anemia with ring sideroblasts while taking isoniazid and has poor nutritional intake. Which reversible mechanism is relevant?', 'Pyridoxine-related impairment of heme synthesis',[
 ('Universal absence of body iron stores','Ring sideroblasts reflect iron accumulation rather than proving absent stores.'),('An ALK rearrangement as the obligatory cause','That is unrelated to this reversible heme-synthesis problem.'),('Excess vitamin B12 as the established mechanism','That does not explain the isoniazid association.')],
 'A ring sideroblast is not automatically a myelodysplastic neoplasm', '**Isoniazid can interfere with vitamin B6-dependent heme synthesis.**',['Nutritional status can increase vulnerability.','Other reversible causes include alcohol, copper deficiency and selected drugs.'], '**Review the exposure and provide cause-specific correction.**',['Coordinate TB treatment changes rather than stopping an essential regimen without a plan.','Monitor hematological response and investigate persistent abnormalities.'],[['Drug clue','Isoniazid'],['Pathway','Pyridoxine-dependent heme synthesis'],['Next step','Correct reversible cause and reassess']],[SI],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has elevated ferritin and persistently high transferrin saturation. What is the appropriate interpretation?', 'Investigate iron overload while considering liver disease, low transferrin and other confounders',[
 ('Ferritin elevation alone always confirms hereditary hemochromatosis','Ferritin has many non-overload causes.'),('High saturation proves a specific HFE genotype','Genotype requires testing.'),('Iron deficiency is established by high saturation','This is not the usual deficient pattern.')],
 'Biochemical evidence should lead to an etiological assessment', '**High TSAT is an important clue to increased circulating iron loading.**',['Ferritin contributes storage information but is inflammation-sensitive.','Liver injury and low transferrin can complicate interpretation.'], '**Use genotype and tissue-iron assessment appropriately.**',['Ancestry and family history affect the likely cause.','Non-HFE disease and secondary loading remain possible.'],[['TSAT','Iron-loading clue'],['Ferritin','Storage and inflammatory signal'],['Etiology','Genetic or secondary assessment']],[HH],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the central regulatory defect in typical HFE-related hereditary hemochromatosis?', 'Inappropriately low hepcidin activity relative to the iron burden',[
 ('Persistently excessive hepcidin causing complete blockade of absorption','That is closer to an iron-restrictive mechanism.'),('Absence of all transferrin production as the usual HFE mechanism','This is a different rare disorder.'),('Autoimmune destruction of every iron-containing erythrocyte','That is not the HFE iron-loading mechanism.')],
 'The body absorbs more iron than it can safely store', '**Insufficient hepcidin restraint permits excessive ferroportin-mediated iron entry into plasma.**',['Iron can accumulate in liver and other organs.','Genotype penetrance and cofactors influence clinical expression.'], '**Assess actual organ burden rather than genotype alone.**',['Alcohol and metabolic liver disease can modify risk.','Family testing and counseling should follow an appropriate pathway.'],[['Regulation','Insufficient hepcidin response'],['Effect','Excess absorption and iron release'],['Outcome','Progressive organ iron loading in susceptible patients']],[HH])
q('An adult with metabolic liver disease has ferritin 650 ng/mL but transferrin saturation is not elevated. What is the best next principle?', 'Evaluate inflammatory and liver-related causes and establish whether true iron overload is present before depletion therapy',[
 ('Diagnose HFE hemochromatosis solely from ferritin','The value is not specific for iron overload.'),('Begin weekly phlebotomy without assessing Hb or iron burden','This can be inappropriate and cause anemia.'),('Assume high ferritin can never coexist with iron overload','Overlap is possible and may need clarification.')],
 'Hyperferritinemia and iron overload are not interchangeable', '**Ferritin rises with metabolic disease, inflammation and cellular injury.**',['TSAT and the clinical phenotype refine the differential.','MRI can quantify liver iron when needed.'], '**Treat the demonstrated mechanism.**',['Manage the liver and metabolic disease.','Do not use depletion therapy as a response to an unexplained laboratory number alone.'],[['Ferritin','Nonspecific elevation possible'],['TSAT / MRI','Clarify loading phenotype'],['Treatment','Based on established cause and burden']],[HH],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A nonanemic patient has confirmed iron-loading hereditary hemochromatosis suitable for treatment. Which is first-line iron depletion?', 'Therapeutic phlebotomy',[
 ('Routine red-cell transfusion','This adds iron.'),('Oral iron supplementation','This increases the iron burden.'),('Erythropoietin monotherapy as the standard depletion treatment','It is not the usual primary strategy.')],
 'Phlebotomy removes iron through red-cell loss', '**Repeated venesection mobilizes stored iron into new erythrocytes.**',['Check Hb and tolerance before procedures.','EASL uses a low-ferritin induction target and maintenance generally around 50–100 micrograms/L.'], '**Avoid excessive depletion and individualize maintenance.**',['The rate of reaccumulation varies.','Treatment does not necessarily reverse established joint disease or all advanced organ injury.'],[['First line','Phlebotomy when suitable'],['Monitoring','Hb, ferritin and tolerance'],['Maintenance','Individual reaccumulation rate']],[HH],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why is routine phlebotomy often unsuitable for transfusion-dependent anemic patients with iron overload?', 'It removes needed red cells, so an appropriate iron-chelation strategy is generally required instead',[
 ('Transfused red cells contain no iron','Repeated transfusions add substantial iron.'),('Chelation increases intestinal iron absorption as its purpose','Its purpose is removal of excess iron.'),('Ferritin alone gives a complete measure of cardiac iron in every patient','Organ-specific assessment may require MRI.')],
 'The source of loading and the ability to tolerate red-cell loss determine treatment', '**Chronic transfusion can produce secondary iron overload.**',['An ongoing anemia prevents a simple venesection approach.','Liver and cardiac MRI can contribute to burden assessment.'], '**Choose and monitor chelation according to the underlying disease.**',['Agent-specific renal, hepatic and hematological toxicities matter.','Follow organ burden rather than one ferritin value alone.'],[['Hereditary loading without anemia','Phlebotomy often suitable'],['Transfusion-dependent anemia','Chelation pathway'],['Monitoring','Organ iron and treatment safety']],[HH,SI])
q('A patient with hemochromatosis-related cirrhosis achieves iron depletion. What is the appropriate long-term principle?', 'Continue indicated hepatocellular-carcinoma surveillance because cirrhosis-related risk can persist',[
 ('Stop all liver follow-up once ferritin is normal','Iron depletion does not automatically eliminate advanced-fibrosis risk.'),('Normal ferritin proves the liver has returned to normal architecture','Biochemical depletion and fibrosis regression are different outcomes.'),('Give prophylactic iron to prevent any future surveillance need','This does not address cancer risk.')],
 'Removing the driver does not instantly remove established organ risk', '**Advanced fibrosis or cirrhosis warrants ongoing risk-based liver care.**',['Assess portal-hypertension and other cirrhosis complications.','Maintain an appropriate iron-monitoring plan.'], '**Distinguish treatment response from complete disease reversal.**',['Some fibrosis can regress but this must not be assumed.','Use the relevant hepatology surveillance pathway.'],[['Iron target','Biochemical depletion'],['Liver architecture','May remain abnormal'],['Follow-up','Continue indicated HCC surveillance']],[HH],style='clinical_scenario')

